4	/// The name of the built-in tool that loads a skill.
5	///
6	/// Here rather than in `application`, because the system prompt names it too and
7	/// `infra::template` cannot reach an application constant without inverting the
8	/// dependency. It was a literal in that template, so renaming this would have told every
9	/// model, in every prompt, to call a tool that no longer exists -- and it would have
10	/// called it, received "Unknown tool", and loaded no skill, with nothing failing at
11	/// build time. A rename is therefore a pairing, not a release: the template teaching
12	/// this name and the core dispatching it ship together.
13	///
14	/// A tool the core owns is named the way models say it. There is no namespace in front
15	/// of it: the `server__` prefix that MCP tools carry exists to keep one server's tools
16	/// from colliding with another's, and a built-in has no server to collide with.
17	pub const LOAD_SKILL_TOOL: &str = "load_skill";
18	
19	/// The name of that tool's one argument.
20	///
21	/// Here rather than in `application` for the same reason as the tool name above, and
22	/// shared for a sharper one: it is spelled in two places that cannot see each other --
23	/// the schema `application::tools` declares, and the corrected call
24	/// [`skill_called_as_tool_message`] writes out for a model to copy. They drifted. The
25	/// message went on teaching `{"name": ...}` after the schema became `skill_name` and
26	/// every other spelling stopped being accepted, so following the message exactly was a
27	/// refusal.
28	///
29	/// Nothing caught it: the argument key is a JSON string built on one side and a JSON
30	/// string built on the other, and the tests below assert the message names the tool and
31	/// the skill, never the key. One constant is what makes the two agree.
32	pub const LOAD_SKILL_ARGUMENT: &str = "skill_name";
